use std
into extra.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace extra). It reads Jupyter notebooks, iCalendar files, vCard files and source code into the document model. Every module-level name starts with `ex_` or `EX_`. All appends to the document model go through `dm::dm_add`, `dm::dm_page`, `dm::dm_set_meta` and `dm::dm_warn_add`; block kinds are `dm::DM_HEAD`, `dm::DM_PARA`, `dm::DM_NOTE`, `dm::DM_FIELD` and `dm::DM_CODE`. The text helpers of the textfmt module are reused: `textfmt::tx_norm(data, out)` (clears out and writes data as UTF-8 with every line break turned into one newline byte), `textfmt::tx_binary(s)`, `textfmt::tx_blank(s)` (true for an empty line or one of spaces and tabs only), `textfmt::tx_trim(s)`, `textfmt::tx_text(data)` (reads Markdown into the model), and the line splitter that works on the module variable `textfmt::tx_src`: `textfmt::tx_lines()` fills `textfmt::tx_ls` and `textfmt::tx_le` (start and end of every line) and `textfmt::tx_line(k)` is line k. The JSON reader of textfmt is reused too: `textfmt::tx_jparse(s)` returns the root node index or -1; a node is `textfmt::tx_jn` at that index, a `TxNode` with `kind` (1 object, 2 array, 3 string, 4 number, 5 true, 6 false, 7 null), `first` (its first child, -1 for none) and `next` (the next sibling, -1 for none); `textfmt::tx_jfind(obj, key)` is the member of an object node or -1; `textfmt::tx_jtext(node, out)` appends the plain text of a scalar node.

```amni
import dm;
import textfmt;
```

`ex_cut(s const byte[:], max int) const byte[:]` is s itself when it has at most max bytes; otherwise the first max bytes, moved back so that the cut never falls inside a UTF-8 sequence (while the byte at the cut position is a continuation byte, 128 to 191, move the cut back by one).

`ex_lower(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends s to out with ASCII letters lower-cased. `ex_upper(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends it with ASCII letters upper-cased.

`ex_unesc(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends s to out with text escapes undone: a backslash followed by "n" or "N" becomes a newline byte (10), and a backslash followed by any other character becomes just that character (so "\," is a comma, "\;" a semicolon and "\\\\" a backslash); a lone trailing backslash is dropped.

`ex_hex(c byte) int` is the value of a hexadecimal digit (either case), or -1.

`ex_qp(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends s to out with quoted-printable undone: an equals sign followed by two hexadecimal digits becomes that byte; an equals sign followed by a newline byte is dropped together with the newline; anything else is copied as is. The result is then converted by `dm::dm_to_utf8` (decoded into a temporary buffer first), so Latin-1 text is repaired.

Line preparation for calendars and contacts. `ex_prep(data const byte[:]) bool` converts data with `textfmt::tx_norm` into a temporary buffer and returns false when `textfmt::tx_binary` says it is binary. Otherwise it writes the unfolded text into `textfmt::tx_src` and calls `textfmt::tx_lines()`, and returns true. Unfolding: the text is read line by line; a physical line that starts with a space or a tab continues the previous logical line (without that first character); also when the previous logical line so far ends with an equals sign and its text before the first colon holds "QUOTED-PRINTABLE" in any letter case, that equals sign is removed and the next physical line continues it as it is (a quoted-printable soft line break). Logical lines are joined with newline bytes.

`ex_prop(line const byte[:], name byte[>..]&, params byte[>..]&, value byte[>..]&) bool` splits a content line of the form NAME;PARAMS:VALUE: it clears the three outputs, finds the first colon that is not inside double quotes (return false when there is none), the name is the text before the first semicolon or that colon (whichever comes first) upper-cased and with any group prefix (the text up to and including the last period in the name) removed, the params are what lies between that first semicolon and the colon (empty when none, kept as written), and the value is everything after the colon. Return true.

`ex_param(params const byte[:], key const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` looks for the parameter KEY=VALUE in params, where parameters are separated by semicolons outside double quotes and the key is compared ignoring letter case; it appends the value without its surrounding double quotes to out and returns true, or returns false when there is none.

`ex_types(params const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and writes the type words of a contact property: the comma separated values of every TYPE parameter and every bare parameter that has no equals sign and is not one of ENCODING, CHARSET, QUOTED-PRINTABLE, BASE64, 8BIT, UTF-8 or VALUE words (compare without letter case), lower-cased, skipping "internet" and "voice" and empty words, joined with ", ".

`ex_value(params const byte[:], raw const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends the text value of a property: when params has an ENCODING parameter equal to QUOTED-PRINTABLE (any letter case) or a bare parameter QUOTED-PRINTABLE, raw is first decoded with `ex_qp`; the result goes through `ex_unesc`.

`ex_flat(s const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends s to out with every newline byte (10) replaced by a space.

`ex_split(s const byte[:], sep byte, k int, out byte[>..]&)` appends to out the k-th field (counting from 0) of s split at the byte sep, not counting a sep that is preceded by a backslash; it appends nothing when there is no such field.

`ex_date(value const byte[:], tzid const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends a readable date: for 8 digits (YYYYMMDD) "YYYY-MM-DD"; for 8 digits, the letter T and 4 or more digits (HHMM or HHMMSS) "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM" (seconds are dropped), followed by " UTC" when the value ends with the letter Z, or otherwise by a space and tzid when tzid is not empty. A value of any other shape is appended as written. A leading "VALUE=DATE:" is not part of value (the parameters are kept apart).

`ex_who(params const byte[:], value const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends a person for an ORGANIZER or ATTENDEE property: the address is value with a leading "mailto:" (any letter case) removed; when params has a CN parameter (`ex_param`) with a non-empty value the person is that name, a space, a less-than sign, the address and a greater-than sign; otherwise just the address.

State of the calendar reader: module variables `var ex_cname`, `ex_csum`, `ex_cwhen`, `ex_cend`, `ex_cloc`, `ex_corg`, `ex_cwho`, `ex_cstat`, `ex_cdesc` (each a growable byte list: summary, start text, end text, location, organizer, attendees joined by ", ", status, description) and `var ex_ckind` (a byte list holding "VEVENT" or "VTODO").

`ex_cal_emit()` adds the component that has been collected: a `dm::DM_HEAD` block of level 2 with the summary (or "(untitled)" when it is empty), then a `dm::DM_FIELD` block of level 0 for each of these that is not empty, in this order: "Start: " and the start text; "End: " and the end text for an event or "Due: " and the end text for a task (the kind is "VTODO"); "Location: "; "Organizer: "; "Attendees: "; "Status: "; then a `dm::DM_PARA` block with the description. Every list is cleared afterwards. The text of a `dm::DM_FIELD` block is first passed through `ex_flat`, so a field never holds a newline.

`ex_ics(data const byte[:]) bool` reads an iCalendar file. Prepare the lines with `ex_prep` (return false when it does). Walk the lines with `textfmt::tx_line` and `ex_prop` (a line that does not split is skipped), keeping a depth counter: BEGIN with the value VCALENDAR is noted (seen true); BEGIN with VEVENT or VTODO at depth 0 starts a component (clear the state lists, store the kind) and sets depth to 1; BEGIN of anything else while depth is at least 1 adds 1 to depth, and its END takes it away again, so nested parts such as VALARM are ignored; END with VEVENT or VTODO at depth 1 calls `ex_cal_emit()`, counts the component and sets depth back to 0. While depth is exactly 1 the properties fill the state: SUMMARY (`ex_value` into the summary), DTSTART (start text via `ex_date` with the TZID parameter), DTEND or DUE (end text), LOCATION, ORGANIZER (`ex_who` of its params and the raw value), ATTENDEE (each appends `ex_who` to the attendees, with ", " between them), STATUS (upper-case text as written), DESCRIPTION. At depth 0 the property X-WR-CALNAME sets the meta "title" (through `ex_value`). At the end call `dm::dm_set_meta("events", n)` with the number of components as decimal text and return true when `ex_ics` saw VCALENDAR or emitted at least one component, otherwise false.

State of the contact reader: module variables `var ex_vfn`, `ex_vn`, `ex_vorg` (byte lists) and `var ex_vf` (a byte list of field lines: each is a letter A to H which gives the field's output order, then the text of the field line, then a newline byte).

`ex_vcf_emit()` adds the card collected: the heading text is the FN when it is not empty, otherwise the formatted N, otherwise the organization, otherwise "(unnamed contact)"; add it as a `dm::DM_HEAD` block of level 2. Then, for each letter from A to H in order, add each field line of `ex_vf` that starts with that letter as a `dm::DM_FIELD` block of level 0 with the rest of the line. Then clear `ex_vfn`, `ex_vn`, `ex_vorg` and `ex_vf`. The field lines are stored by `ex_vcf`.

`ex_vcf(data const byte[:]) bool` reads a vCard file (versions 2.1, 3.0 and 4.0, one or many cards). Prepare the lines with `ex_prep` (return false when it does). Walk the lines with `textfmt::tx_line` and `ex_prop`: BEGIN with the value VCARD starts a card (clear the state); END with VCARD calls `ex_vcf_emit()` and counts the card; the properties of a card (compare the upper-cased value of BEGIN and END without letter case) fill the state, with the value from `ex_value` (use `ex_split` for the structured ones, taking the raw value and the unescaping per part): FN into `ex_vfn`; N (the parts family, given, additional, prefix, suffix, unescaped; the formatted name is prefix, given, additional, family, suffix, the non-empty ones joined by spaces) into `ex_vn`; ORG (its parts joined by ", ") into `ex_vorg` and also the field "Organization: " and the value with letter A; TITLE with letter B and the label "Title: "; TEL with letter C and the label "Phone: " (a leading "tel:" in any letter case removed from the value); EMAIL with letter D and the label "Email: " (a leading "mailto:" removed); ADR with letter E and the label "Address: " (the parts pobox, extended, street, city, region, postal code and country, non-empty ones joined by ", "); URL with letter F and "URL: "; BDAY with letter G and "Birthday: " (a value of 8 digits is written YYYY-MM-DD; any other value is cut at a letter T); NOTE with letter H and "Note: ". For TEL, EMAIL and ADR append to the value a space, an opening parenthesis, the type words from `ex_types` and a closing parenthesis when the type words are not empty. A field line is its letter, its label, its value passed through `ex_flat` and a newline byte; skip fields whose value is empty. At the end call `dm::dm_set_meta("contacts", n)` with the number of cards as decimal text. Return true when at least one card was found, otherwise false.

Notebooks. `ex_jget(node int, key const byte[:]) int` is `textfmt::tx_jfind` of the node and key, or -1 when node is -1 or not an object. `ex_jtxt(node int, out byte[>..]&)` appends the text of a string node, or for an array node the texts of its string elements one after another with nothing between them (as notebooks store multi-line text); other nodes append nothing. `ex_jstr(node int, key const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` appends `ex_jtxt` of the member and returns true when the member exists.

`ex_out(text const byte[:])` adds one notebook output: when the text is longer than 4000 bytes it is cut with `ex_cut` to 4000 bytes and the warning "notebook output truncated at 4000 bytes" is added with `dm::dm_warn_add`; then it is added as a `dm::DM_CODE` block of level 0 (empty text adds nothing).

`ex_nb_cell(cell int, v3 bool, lang byte[>..]&)` reads one notebook cell. Its type is the text of the member "cell_type". For "markdown": the text of the member "source" is read with `textfmt::tx_text`. For "heading" (version 3 only): a `dm::DM_HEAD` block with the text of "source" and the level from the number member "level" (1 when missing, and never more than the highest heading level `dm` allows). For "raw": the source added as a `dm::DM_PARA` block. For "code": the number member "execution_count" (version 3: "prompt_number") when it is a number (kind 4) adds a `dm::DM_NOTE` block "In [" and the number as written and "]"; then the text of "source" (version 3: "input") is added as a `dm::DM_CODE` block (when lang is still empty and the cell has a string member "language", copy it into lang); then every element of the member "outputs" in order: its "output_type" is "stream" for text output (the member "text" goes through `ex_out`); "execute_result" or "display_data" (version 4: the member "text/plain" of the member "data"; version 3 uses "pyout" for results and "display_data", whose plain text is the member "text") goes through `ex_out`; "error" (version 3: "pyerr") goes through `ex_out` as the member "ename", a colon, a space and the member "evalue" (just the name when there is no value). Cells of other types add nothing.

`ex_ipynb(data const byte[:]) bool` reads a Jupyter notebook: parse data with `textfmt::tx_jparse` and return false unless the root is an object (kind 1) that has either a member "cells" (nbformat 4; an array of cells) or a member "worksheets" (nbformat 3; an array of worksheets, each with its own member "cells"). Go through all cells in order with `ex_nb_cell` (v3 true for worksheets). The notebook's language is the first non-empty text among: metadata.kernelspec.language, metadata.language_info.name, metadata.language, metadata.kernelspec.name, and the language found in a code cell by `ex_nb_cell`; set the meta "language" to it. Also set the meta "nbformat" to the number member "nbformat" as written (when there is one) and the meta "cells" to the number of cells as decimal text. Return true.

Source code. `ex_is_code_ext(ext const byte[:]) bool` is true when ext, with one leading period removed and lower-cased, is one of: py, rs, go, js, mjs, cjs, ts, tsx, jsx, c, h, cc, cpp, hpp, cs, java, kt, kts, swift, rb, php, lua, pl, r, m, scala, sh, bash, zsh, fish, ps1, bat, sql, he, amni, gold, zig, nim, dart, ex, exs, erl, hs, ml, fs, v, sv, vhd, cmake, gradle, make, mk, dockerfile, proto, graphql, vue, svelte, css, scss, less.

`ex_langname(ext const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` appends the language name for an extension (already lower-cased, no period): py python, rs rust, go go, js mjs cjs and jsx javascript, ts and tsx typescript, c and h c, cc cpp and hpp cpp, cs csharp, java java, kt and kts kotlin, swift swift, rb ruby, php php, lua lua, pl perl, r r, m objective-c, scala scala, sh bash and zsh shell, fish fish, ps1 powershell, bat batch, sql sql, he amni and gold helium, zig zig, nim nim, dart dart, ex and exs elixir, erl erlang, hs haskell, ml ocaml, fs fsharp, v sv and vhd verilog, cmake cmake, gradle gradle, make and mk make, dockerfile dockerfile, proto protobuf, graphql graphql, vue vue, svelte svelte, css scss and less css; any other extension is appended as it is.

`ex_word(s const byte[:], at int) int` returns the index where the run of letters, digits, underscores and dollar signs that starts at at ends (at itself when there is none).

`ex_skip(s const byte[:], at int) int` returns the first index from at that is not a space or a tab.

`ex_comment(line const byte[:]) bool` is true when the line, ignoring leading spaces and tabs, starts with "//", "/*", "*", "#", "@", "--" or a triple double quote, or is blank.

`ex_isdef(line const byte[:], lang const byte[:]) bool` says whether the line starts a definition. Let t be the line from its first byte that is not a space or tab. Repeatedly remove a leading modifier word together with the spaces after it, while the first word of t is one of: pub (also in the form pub followed by a parenthesised part such as "(crate)"), export, default, async, static, public, private, protected, internal, final, abstract, open, override, extern, unsafe, inline, virtual, sealed, partial, data, suspend, readonly, declare, or const when the word after it is fn or function or async. Let w be the first word that remains (`ex_word`). Rule 1: w is one of def, class, fn, struct, enum, impl, trait, mod, func, type, function, interface, namespace, object, protocol, extension, record, module, union, fun, sub, proc, typedef, and the byte after w is a less-than sign, or spaces follow it and then a letter, an underscore, a dollar sign or an asterisk, or (for func and function only) an opening parenthesis: true. Rule 2: w is const, let or var and the line contains "=>" or contains the word "function" after an equals sign: true. Rule 3: t is an identifier followed by optional spaces and "()" and then nothing or optional spaces and an opening brace: true. Rule 4, only when lang is one of c, h, cc, cpp, hpp, cs, java, kt, kts, swift: true when t contains an opening parenthesis, has at least one identifier character before it, the part before it contains no equals sign, does not start with one of if, for, while, switch, return, else, do, catch, try, new, throw, case, using, sizeof, delete or a hash sign, t does not end with a semicolon or a comma, and the part before the parenthesis holds at least two words or one word that starts with an upper-case letter. Otherwise false.

`ex_head(a int, b int, lang const byte[:]) int` looks at the lines a up to b minus 1 of `textfmt::tx_src` (at most the first 10 of them), skips lines for which `ex_comment` is true, and returns the index of the first other line when `ex_isdef` holds for it, otherwise -1.

`ex_top(k int) bool` is true when line k of `textfmt::tx_src` is not blank and does not start with a space or a tab. `ex_bound(k int) bool` is true when k is above 0, line k minus 1 is blank (`textfmt::tx_blank`) and `ex_top(k)` holds.

`ex_split_at(start int, n int, lang const byte[:]) int` returns the exclusive end line of the block that begins at line start, among n lines in all. A block has at most 60 lines. For k from start plus 1 up to start plus 60: when k equals n the block ends there (return n); when `ex_bound(k)` holds and `ex_head(k, min(k plus 10, n), lang)` is not -1 (a definition begins at k) return k; when `ex_bound(k)` holds otherwise remember k as the soft candidate. When the loop ends without a return: return the soft candidate if there is one; otherwise the largest k in that range for which line k minus 1 is blank, if there is one; otherwise start plus 60 (capped at n).

`ex_code(data const byte[:], lang const byte[:]) bool` reads source code. Let ext be lang lower-cased with one leading period removed. Convert data with `textfmt::tx_norm` into `textfmt::tx_src`, return false when `textfmt::tx_binary` says it is binary, call `textfmt::tx_lines()` and let n be the number of lines. Set the meta "language" to `ex_langname` of ext and "lines" to n as decimal text. Then start at line 0 and, while start is below n: let e be `ex_split_at(start, n, ext)`; when the lines start to e minus 1 are not all blank, call `dm::dm_page(start plus 1, "")` (the first line number of the block), then, when h is `ex_head(start, e, ext)` and not -1, add a `dm::DM_HEAD` block of level 3 with the signature: line h without leading and trailing spaces and tabs, with one trailing opening brace (and the spaces before it) removed, cut to 120 bytes by `ex_cut`; then add the text from the start of line start to the end of line e minus 1 (a slice of `textfmt::tx_src`) as one `dm::DM_CODE` block of level 0. Continue at e. Return true.
